1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,960
Meet Theo.

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:04,197
He's an engineer in an old factory

3
00:00:04,197 --> 00:00:05,193
town in Europe,

4
00:00:05,193 --> 00:00:07,227
and everyone keeps telling him the same

5
00:00:07,227 --> 00:00:08,030
thing.

6
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:10,360
Europe is falling behind.

7
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:12,240
America has the tech giants.

8
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:13,980
China has the factories.

9
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:15,597
Europe has aging towns,

10
00:00:15,597 --> 00:00:19,010
pricey energy and a lot of nostalgia.

11
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:21,041
But Theo's town still makes parts for

12
00:00:21,041 --> 00:00:23,044
machines the whole world needs,

13
00:00:23,044 --> 00:00:25,745
and his sister Nora works on medicines

14
00:00:25,745 --> 00:00:27,470
sold around the world.

15
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:29,692
So here's the question Theo is about

16
00:00:29,692 --> 00:00:30,690
to chase.

17
00:00:30,770 --> 00:00:32,721
What if the technology that seems to

18
00:00:32,721 --> 00:00:34,767
threaten Europe could bring it back?

19
00:00:35,217 --> 00:00:42,133
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

20
00:00:53,784 --> 00:00:55,588
Start with what Theo sees from his

21
00:00:55,588 --> 00:00:56,704
window.

22
00:00:56,794 --> 00:00:58,754
Europe is not poor.

23
00:00:58,754 --> 00:00:59,719
By the IMF's count,

24
00:00:59,719 --> 00:01:02,082
the European Union's economy is worth

25
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:04,229
about twenty-one trillion dollars a year,

26
00:01:04,229 --> 00:01:06,364
a little bigger than China's.

27
00:01:06,364 --> 00:01:07,704
It's a giant.

28
00:01:07,704 --> 00:01:10,134
It's just a giant that keeps slipping.

29
00:01:10,164 --> 00:01:12,028
Theo's town has a hospital,

30
00:01:12,028 --> 00:01:13,916
good schools and a train that mostly

31
00:01:13,916 --> 00:01:15,184
runs on time.

32
00:01:15,184 --> 00:01:17,410
That's what a rich place looks like.

33
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:19,300
Slipping how?

34
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:21,866
A report for the European Commission,

35
00:01:21,866 --> 00:01:24,164
led by the former central banker Mario

36
00:01:24,164 --> 00:01:24,770
Draghi,

37
00:01:24,770 --> 00:01:27,277
found the gap between Europe and America

38
00:01:27,277 --> 00:01:29,310
has been widening for years.

39
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:31,070
And most of it comes down to

40
00:01:31,070 --> 00:01:32,550
a single thing.

41
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:35,330
European workers simply produce less in

42
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:37,402
the same amount of time.

43
00:01:37,650 --> 00:01:39,601
Theo sees it on his own shop

44
00:01:39,601 --> 00:01:40,480
floor.

45
00:01:40,570 --> 00:01:42,345
The veterans are retiring,

46
00:01:42,345 --> 00:01:44,540
and fewer young people walk in.

47
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:46,854
Draghi's report warns that Europe will

48
00:01:46,854 --> 00:01:49,270
soon have just two workers for every

49
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:50,595
retiree.

50
00:01:50,850 --> 00:01:53,040
Then there's the power bill.

51
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:54,951
European companies pay two to three times

52
00:01:54,951 --> 00:01:56,544
what American companies pay for

53
00:01:56,544 --> 00:01:57,940
electricity.

54
00:01:58,060 --> 00:01:59,802
And when Europe grows a promising tech

55
00:01:59,802 --> 00:02:00,308
startup,

56
00:02:00,308 --> 00:02:01,876
it often packs up and moves to

57
00:02:01,876 --> 00:02:03,870
America to raise money.

58
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:05,614
Theo's boss has a word for all

59
00:02:05,614 --> 00:02:06,580
of this.

60
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:08,055
Squeezed.

61
00:02:08,317 --> 00:02:10,437
So that's the paradox.

62
00:02:10,567 --> 00:02:12,109
A rich continent with world-class

63
00:02:12,109 --> 00:02:14,404
universities and famous companies,

64
00:02:14,404 --> 00:02:17,227
and a future everyone is worried about.

65
00:02:17,227 --> 00:02:18,917
Those are the facts.

66
00:02:18,917 --> 00:02:21,055
Now comes Theo's hunch.

67
00:02:25,717 --> 00:02:28,957
The old recipe for growth was simple.

68
00:02:28,967 --> 00:02:32,038
More workers plus more machines makes more

69
00:02:32,038 --> 00:02:32,917
stuff.

70
00:02:32,987 --> 00:02:35,887
Europe's industrial revolution ran on it.

71
00:02:35,927 --> 00:02:37,772
Theo's great-grandfather worked in this

72
00:02:37,772 --> 00:02:38,982
very factory,

73
00:02:38,982 --> 00:02:40,827
shoulder to shoulder with hundreds of

74
00:02:40,827 --> 00:02:41,837
others.

75
00:02:41,987 --> 00:02:42,753
Back then,

76
00:02:42,753 --> 00:02:45,731
Europe was the workshop of the world.

77
00:02:45,984 --> 00:02:48,484
Let me draw Theo's problem.

78
00:02:48,484 --> 00:02:50,730
Output equals the number of workers,

79
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:53,184
times what each worker produces.

80
00:02:53,244 --> 00:02:55,403
If the number of workers starts falling,

81
00:02:55,403 --> 00:02:57,165
and in Europe it's about to,

82
00:02:57,165 --> 00:02:58,948
there's just a single way to keep

83
00:02:58,948 --> 00:02:59,944
growing.

84
00:02:59,954 --> 00:03:02,866
Each worker has to produce more.

85
00:03:03,117 --> 00:03:05,987
So Theo asks the obvious question.

86
00:03:06,007 --> 00:03:08,473
What if technology let his factory make

87
00:03:08,473 --> 00:03:10,717
far more with far fewer people?

88
00:03:10,997 --> 00:03:12,977
Then a shrinking workforce stops being a

89
00:03:12,977 --> 00:03:14,117
death sentence.

90
00:03:14,117 --> 00:03:16,507
And a different question starts to matter.

91
00:03:16,617 --> 00:03:18,380
Theo writes it on the whiteboard in

92
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:19,509
the break room.

93
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:26,950
Theo visits a modern automated plant.

94
00:03:26,950 --> 00:03:29,710
Cameras running AI check every part and

95
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:31,230
guide robot arms.

96
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:33,190
The industry's robot federation calls that

97
00:03:33,190 --> 00:03:34,970
kind of machine vision industry-grade.

98
00:03:34,970 --> 00:03:36,480
Sensors warn before a motor fails.

99
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:39,340
Self-driving carts move parts.

100
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:40,240
And engineers test the whole line in

101
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:41,740
a digital twin before building it.

102
00:03:41,790 --> 00:03:45,960
Theo counts the people on the floor.

103
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:48,326
It doesn't take long.

104
00:03:48,584 --> 00:03:50,834
But the tour guide is honest.

105
00:03:50,834 --> 00:03:53,042
Robots that can learn any job are

106
00:03:53,042 --> 00:03:54,624
not ready yet.

107
00:03:54,764 --> 00:03:56,384
So this isn't a factory with no

108
00:03:56,384 --> 00:03:57,344
people.

109
00:03:57,464 --> 00:04:00,106
It's a factory where each person does

110
00:04:00,106 --> 00:04:01,234
far more.

111
00:04:01,404 --> 00:04:03,900
Theo likes the sound of that.

112
00:04:04,150 --> 00:04:07,040
And Europe isn't starting from scratch.

113
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:09,273
Germany already runs more robots per

114
00:04:09,273 --> 00:04:11,690
factory worker than America does,

115
00:04:11,690 --> 00:04:14,117
and far more than the world average.

116
00:04:14,384 --> 00:04:16,684
Here's the mechanism that changes Theo's

117
00:04:16,684 --> 00:04:17,724
thinking.

118
00:04:17,724 --> 00:04:20,864
Picture a factory's costs as a stack.

119
00:04:20,864 --> 00:04:23,454
Automation shrinks the labor slice.

120
00:04:23,454 --> 00:04:24,700
But the machines,

121
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:26,813
the software and the know-how behind them

122
00:04:26,813 --> 00:04:29,314
become a bigger part of the bill.

123
00:04:29,474 --> 00:04:32,164
So the money starts flowing to whoever

124
00:04:32,164 --> 00:04:33,571
owns those things.

125
00:04:33,817 --> 00:04:35,757
Workers don't vanish.

126
00:04:35,757 --> 00:04:37,450
Someone designs the robots,

127
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:38,500
writes the code,

128
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:39,957
fixes the line.

129
00:04:40,127 --> 00:04:42,035
But if fewer hands are needed,

130
00:04:42,035 --> 00:04:43,514
cheap labor matters less,

131
00:04:43,514 --> 00:04:46,687
and owning the technology may matter more.

132
00:04:46,837 --> 00:04:48,564
And Europe already owns a lot of

133
00:04:48,564 --> 00:04:49,327
it.

134
00:04:49,327 --> 00:04:51,938
Nora works in the clearest example.

135
00:04:56,584 --> 00:04:57,804
Medicine.

136
00:04:57,804 --> 00:04:59,554
Here's a number that surprises most

137
00:04:59,554 --> 00:05:00,854
Americans.

138
00:05:00,864 --> 00:05:02,345
Last year the EU sold the world

139
00:05:02,345 --> 00:05:04,460
about two hundred and fifty billion

140
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:06,964
dollars more medicine than it bought.

141
00:05:06,994 --> 00:05:08,484
Its biggest customer?

142
00:05:08,484 --> 00:05:10,202
The United States.

143
00:05:10,450 --> 00:05:13,110
Nora walks Theo past the giants.

144
00:05:13,110 --> 00:05:15,350
Roche and Novartis in Switzerland.

145
00:05:15,350 --> 00:05:17,870
AstraZeneca and GSK in Britain.

146
00:05:17,870 --> 00:05:19,480
Sanofi in France.

147
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,300
Novo Nordisk in Denmark.

148
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:23,610
Switzerland and Britain are in Europe,

149
00:05:23,610 --> 00:05:25,180
but not in the EU.

150
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:27,189
Each of them sells medicine all over

151
00:05:27,189 --> 00:05:28,191
the world.

152
00:05:28,450 --> 00:05:30,358
So where does the money in a

153
00:05:30,358 --> 00:05:32,150
medicine come from?

154
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:33,450
Let me draw it.

155
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:35,510
Research finds a molecule.

156
00:05:35,510 --> 00:05:37,040
Patents protect it.

157
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:39,030
Trials prove it works.

158
00:05:39,030 --> 00:05:40,644
Then factories make it,

159
00:05:40,644 --> 00:05:43,350
and trucks and planes ship it worldwide.

160
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:45,228
Most of the value sits at the

161
00:05:45,228 --> 00:05:45,950
start.

162
00:05:45,950 --> 00:05:47,138
In the ideas,

163
00:05:47,138 --> 00:05:49,194
the patents and the proof.

164
00:05:49,450 --> 00:05:52,312
Nora's favorite example is Novo Nordisk,

165
00:05:52,312 --> 00:05:55,194
the Danish maker of diabetes and obesity

166
00:05:55,194 --> 00:05:55,950
drugs.

167
00:05:55,950 --> 00:05:58,207
It spends almost eight billion dollars a

168
00:05:58,207 --> 00:05:59,580
year on research.

169
00:05:59,580 --> 00:06:01,680
It's expanding its factories too.

170
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:04,004
But what it really sells is decades

171
00:06:04,004 --> 00:06:06,464
of science that only it controls.

172
00:06:06,717 --> 00:06:09,487
Can AI speed that science up?

173
00:06:09,487 --> 00:06:10,147
Nora is cautious.

174
00:06:10,147 --> 00:06:13,281
AstraZeneca bought a company called

175
00:06:13,281 --> 00:06:15,892
Modella AI to build AI into its

176
00:06:15,892 --> 00:06:17,537
cancer research.

177
00:06:17,697 --> 00:06:19,736
But a new medicine still takes more

178
00:06:19,736 --> 00:06:20,735
than a decade,

179
00:06:20,735 --> 00:06:22,346
and for every success,

180
00:06:22,346 --> 00:06:24,577
thousands of candidates fail.

181
00:06:24,757 --> 00:06:26,997
AI may help pick better bets.

182
00:06:26,997 --> 00:06:29,885
It doesn't guarantee a single approval.

183
00:06:30,150 --> 00:06:33,340
And that's Theo's first big lesson.

184
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:35,347
You don't have to make everything at

185
00:06:35,347 --> 00:06:37,080
home to capture the value.

186
00:06:37,110 --> 00:06:38,265
You have to own,

187
00:06:38,265 --> 00:06:39,657
and keep improving,

188
00:06:39,657 --> 00:06:41,907
the thing everyone else needs.

189
00:06:46,550 --> 00:06:49,322
Then Nora shows him the most extreme

190
00:06:49,322 --> 00:06:50,230
case.

191
00:06:50,250 --> 00:06:50,663
ASML,

192
00:06:50,663 --> 00:06:52,600
in the Dutch town of Veldhoven,

193
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:54,994
is the only company that makes the

194
00:06:54,994 --> 00:06:57,170
machines used to print the world's most

195
00:06:57,170 --> 00:06:58,410
advanced chips.

196
00:06:58,410 --> 00:07:01,077
Last year it kept almost eleven billion

197
00:07:01,077 --> 00:07:02,890
dollars in profit.

198
00:07:03,050 --> 00:07:04,140
And it doesn't make a single chip.

199
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:05,681
Theo has to sit down.

200
00:07:05,917 --> 00:07:07,348
It sits in the middle of a

201
00:07:07,348 --> 00:07:08,037
web.

202
00:07:08,037 --> 00:07:10,793
Thousands of suppliers feed in lenses,

203
00:07:10,793 --> 00:07:12,507
lasers and parts.

204
00:07:12,667 --> 00:07:14,708
Out go machines to chip factories in

205
00:07:14,708 --> 00:07:16,607
Asia and America.

206
00:07:16,857 --> 00:07:19,426
Every time anyone builds a factory for

207
00:07:19,426 --> 00:07:20,046
AI chips,

208
00:07:20,046 --> 00:07:21,545
part of the bill lands in the

209
00:07:21,545 --> 00:07:22,787
Netherlands.

210
00:07:22,847 --> 00:07:24,687
Nora calls it Europe's quietest

211
00:07:24,687 --> 00:07:26,105
superpower.

212
00:07:26,350 --> 00:07:29,040
Theo recognizes the next example.

213
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:29,717
Siemens,

214
00:07:29,717 --> 00:07:30,627
from Munich,

215
00:07:30,627 --> 00:07:32,703
sells factories their controllers,

216
00:07:32,703 --> 00:07:34,547
automation and software,

217
00:07:34,547 --> 00:07:36,780
including digital twins.

218
00:07:37,070 --> 00:07:39,503
Its chief executive calls industrial AI an

219
00:07:39,503 --> 00:07:42,130
exceptional growth opportunity.

220
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:43,829
When the world automates,

221
00:07:43,829 --> 00:07:46,289
somebody sells the automation.

222
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:48,426
And then there's Airbus,

223
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:50,930
run from near Toulouse in France.

224
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:52,360
Its real product is coordination.

225
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:54,847
Thousands of parts from many countries,

226
00:07:54,847 --> 00:07:58,338
engineered and certified to fit together,

227
00:07:58,338 --> 00:08:01,450
with years of orders already waiting.

228
00:08:01,770 --> 00:08:04,062
That's exactly where smarter software and

229
00:08:04,062 --> 00:08:06,266
automation could pay off.

230
00:08:06,517 --> 00:08:08,747
Theo stays skeptical.

231
00:08:08,797 --> 00:08:11,247
Europe doesn't dominate everything.

232
00:08:11,287 --> 00:08:13,384
China installs far more robots,

233
00:08:13,384 --> 00:08:15,505
and most cloud and consumer tech is

234
00:08:15,505 --> 00:08:17,047
American.

235
00:08:17,237 --> 00:08:19,764
But Europe owns capabilities that could

236
00:08:19,764 --> 00:08:21,601
gain value as production gets more

237
00:08:21,601 --> 00:08:23,120
automated.

238
00:08:27,784 --> 00:08:30,544
Which brings Theo to the big idea.

239
00:08:30,544 --> 00:08:31,685
For decades,

240
00:08:31,685 --> 00:08:34,246
towns like his competed to host factories

241
00:08:34,246 --> 00:08:35,524
and their jobs.

242
00:08:35,524 --> 00:08:37,364
In a more automated world,

243
00:08:37,364 --> 00:08:40,444
the factory floor may employ fewer people.

244
00:08:40,524 --> 00:08:42,888
And the question shifts from who operates

245
00:08:42,888 --> 00:08:45,207
the machines to who owns them.

246
00:08:45,450 --> 00:08:48,700
So Theo sketches his own company's future.

247
00:08:48,750 --> 00:08:50,480
Call it Company E.

248
00:08:50,490 --> 00:08:52,125
Its engineers in Europe develop the

249
00:08:52,125 --> 00:08:53,310
technology.

250
00:08:53,310 --> 00:08:55,472
It owns the patents and the software,

251
00:08:55,472 --> 00:08:57,873
makes the key decisions and carries the

252
00:08:57,873 --> 00:08:58,910
big risks.

253
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:01,122
Partner factories in other countries make

254
00:09:01,122 --> 00:09:03,110
the product under license.

255
00:09:03,350 --> 00:09:05,079
Now watch the money,

256
00:09:05,079 --> 00:09:07,730
with made-up numbers on screen.

257
00:09:07,810 --> 00:09:10,000
The partner factory earns a small,

258
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,370
steady fee on top of its costs.

259
00:09:12,390 --> 00:09:14,690
Company E keeps what's left.

260
00:09:14,690 --> 00:09:17,226
Then the factory automates,

261
00:09:17,226 --> 00:09:18,850
and its costs fall.

262
00:09:18,980 --> 00:09:20,603
Its fee shrinks a little,

263
00:09:20,603 --> 00:09:23,320
because it's charged on lower costs.

264
00:09:23,330 --> 00:09:25,792
And Company E's share of each sale

265
00:09:25,792 --> 00:09:27,622
grows by more than half.

266
00:09:27,884 --> 00:09:30,344
But there are catches.

267
00:09:30,524 --> 00:09:32,865
This only works if Company E really

268
00:09:32,865 --> 00:09:33,695
does the work,

269
00:09:33,695 --> 00:09:34,951
and if prices hold,

270
00:09:34,951 --> 00:09:37,254
while rivals automate too.

271
00:09:37,324 --> 00:09:39,816
And simply registering a patent somewhere

272
00:09:39,816 --> 00:09:41,354
doesn't move the profit.

273
00:09:41,354 --> 00:09:42,231
The real work,

274
00:09:42,231 --> 00:09:44,480
the people and the decisions have to

275
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:45,529
be there.

276
00:09:45,784 --> 00:09:48,152
So the question for Europe isn't whether

277
00:09:48,152 --> 00:09:50,844
it can win back the assembly line.

278
00:09:50,844 --> 00:09:52,997
It's whether it can attract and keep

279
00:09:52,997 --> 00:09:54,169
the companies,

280
00:09:54,169 --> 00:09:55,102
the scientists,

281
00:09:55,102 --> 00:09:57,494
the money and the ideas that profit

282
00:09:57,494 --> 00:10:00,604
from automated production everywhere.

283
00:10:00,604 --> 00:10:03,474
Theo circles that sentence on his sketch.

284
00:10:03,717 --> 00:10:05,507
Then Theo's boss throws in a twist.

285
00:10:05,507 --> 00:10:07,499
If robots make labor matter less,

286
00:10:07,499 --> 00:10:09,047
other costs matter more.

287
00:10:09,047 --> 00:10:10,827
Energy.

288
00:10:10,827 --> 00:10:11,557
Money.

289
00:10:11,557 --> 00:10:12,217
Logistics.

290
00:10:12,217 --> 00:10:14,157
Stability.

291
00:10:14,157 --> 00:10:16,507
And the taxes on producing.

292
00:10:16,617 --> 00:10:17,145
Brazil,

293
00:10:17,145 --> 00:10:18,037
for example,

294
00:10:18,037 --> 00:10:19,859
gets most of its electricity from

295
00:10:19,859 --> 00:10:21,187
renewables.

296
00:10:21,277 --> 00:10:23,637
Yet an industry study found that almost

297
00:10:23,637 --> 00:10:25,783
half of what its power companies bill

298
00:10:25,783 --> 00:10:27,478
goes to taxes and charges,

299
00:10:27,478 --> 00:10:29,839
and the country is now overhauling its

300
00:10:29,839 --> 00:10:31,297
sales taxes.

301
00:10:31,447 --> 00:10:33,503
So a country with cheap power would

302
00:10:33,503 --> 00:10:36,387
still need competitive costs to produce.

303
00:10:36,397 --> 00:10:38,754
And even if Company E's partner plant

304
00:10:38,754 --> 00:10:40,018
ends up in Brazil,

305
00:10:40,018 --> 00:10:42,375
the profit from the technology could still

306
00:10:42,375 --> 00:10:44,046
come home to Europe.

307
00:10:48,684 --> 00:10:51,043
Can Europe pull in the people and

308
00:10:51,043 --> 00:10:54,244
the money that kind of company needs?

309
00:10:54,244 --> 00:10:56,234
It has real pull.

310
00:10:56,234 --> 00:10:57,017
Strong courts,

311
00:10:57,017 --> 00:10:58,070
research labs,

312
00:10:58,070 --> 00:10:59,934
a huge single market,

313
00:10:59,934 --> 00:11:01,527
industrial clusters,

314
00:11:01,527 --> 00:11:04,558
and cities people want to live in.

315
00:11:04,817 --> 00:11:07,507
But Nora warns him.

316
00:11:07,517 --> 00:11:09,517
Wealthy residents are not the same as

317
00:11:09,517 --> 00:11:11,317
productive investment.

318
00:11:11,317 --> 00:11:13,630
A head office is not a research

319
00:11:13,630 --> 00:11:14,487
lab.

320
00:11:14,527 --> 00:11:18,281
Only real activity builds real growth.

321
00:11:18,517 --> 00:11:20,937
And newcomers bring problems.

322
00:11:20,987 --> 00:11:22,287
Pricier housing.

323
00:11:22,287 --> 00:11:23,817
Wider inequality.

324
00:11:23,817 --> 00:11:26,037
Shortages of skilled workers.

325
00:11:26,037 --> 00:11:28,596
Rich arrivals don't automatically pay for

326
00:11:28,596 --> 00:11:30,377
pensions and hospitals.

327
00:11:30,447 --> 00:11:32,300
That depends on what they do and

328
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:33,827
how they're taxed.

329
00:11:33,847 --> 00:11:35,957
Nora has watched the rents climb in

330
00:11:35,957 --> 00:11:37,730
her own neighborhood.

331
00:11:42,384 --> 00:11:44,858
So what would a comeback take,

332
00:11:44,858 --> 00:11:47,404
for Theo's town and for Europe?

333
00:11:47,454 --> 00:11:48,714
Let me list it.

334
00:11:48,714 --> 00:11:51,544
Let companies grow big without leaving.

335
00:11:51,544 --> 00:11:53,784
Make money easier to raise.

336
00:11:53,784 --> 00:11:55,704
Bring down energy costs.

337
00:11:55,704 --> 00:11:58,529
Actually use AI in everyday factories and

338
00:11:58,529 --> 00:11:59,704
offices.

339
00:11:59,704 --> 00:12:01,974
And compete hard for talent.

340
00:12:01,974 --> 00:12:02,625
Miss these,

341
00:12:02,625 --> 00:12:05,020
and the profits flow elsewhere.

342
00:12:05,284 --> 00:12:05,859
So,

343
00:12:05,859 --> 00:12:09,184
could AI bring Europe back to power?

344
00:12:09,354 --> 00:12:11,794
Not by building the most factories.

345
00:12:11,794 --> 00:12:13,694
That race is China's.

346
00:12:13,824 --> 00:12:16,524
And not by copying Silicon Valley.

347
00:12:16,794 --> 00:12:18,062
But as the owner,

348
00:12:18,062 --> 00:12:19,115
developer,

349
00:12:19,115 --> 00:12:21,909
financier and licensor of technology the

350
00:12:21,909 --> 00:12:23,584
whole world runs on.

351
00:12:23,744 --> 00:12:25,289
It's a plausible path,

352
00:12:25,289 --> 00:12:26,784
not a promise.

353
00:12:26,944 --> 00:12:28,824
Europe has the assets.

354
00:12:28,824 --> 00:12:31,188
The rest depends on choices it hasn't

355
00:12:31,188 --> 00:12:32,313
made yet.

356
00:12:32,550 --> 00:12:33,758
For generations,

357
00:12:33,758 --> 00:12:36,010
economic power meant factories and

358
00:12:36,010 --> 00:12:36,990
workers.

359
00:12:36,990 --> 00:12:38,593
In a world run more and more

360
00:12:38,593 --> 00:12:39,715
by machines,

361
00:12:39,715 --> 00:12:41,440
the question may change.

362
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:43,260
Not just who makes the products,

363
00:12:43,260 --> 00:12:45,080
but who owns the technology,

364
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:47,930
the machines and the ideas behind them.

365
00:12:48,150 --> 00:12:50,084
And for Theo's old factory town,

366
00:12:50,084 --> 00:12:52,256
that could be a very different way

367
00:12:52,256 --> 00:12:53,942
back into the race.

368
00:12:54,184 --> 00:12:54,781
Next time,

369
00:12:54,781 --> 00:12:57,457
we follow the money behind another hidden

370
00:12:57,457 --> 00:12:59,654
link of the world economy.

371
00:12:59,654 --> 00:13:01,761
This is The Hidden Economics.
